{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "203",
    "case_summary": "A 20-year-old unmarried girl, deaf and mute since birth, presented with a 6-month history of a slowly enlarging left vulvar swelling that had become extremely painful during the past week. She had no fever or vaginal discharge. On examination, her vital signs were stable and she was afebrile, with no inguinal lymphadenopathy. Local examination showed a left labial swelling with signs of inflammation, and a clinical diagnosis of Bartholin’s abscess was made; she was started on parenteral antibiotics. Within 24 hours, her pain decreased and the inflammation subsided. Because the patient and her parents insisted that she was sexually naïve, the lesion was reexamined. Now noted was a 5 × 5 cm swelling of the left labia majora without signs of inflammation; the mass was firm (not cystic), the labia minora were not everted, and the hymen was intact.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Epitheloid leiomyoma\nB. Bartholin’s abscess\nC. fibroma\nD. lymphangioma\nE. soft\nF. neurogenic tumor\nG. Initial consideration of Bartholin’s abscess\nH. Exclusion of Bartholin’s cyst\n",
